MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The blocker on an industrial pilot is not price and it is not features. It is
the eleven weeks it takes to get an unknown tool past a plant's IT department.
Everything built so far assumes somebody already decided to adopt it; nothing
ran before that decision.

The kit runs before it. An engineer copies a folder onto a machine already
inside the plant, edits two files, runs one command, and gets back a sealed
evidence ledger, an HTML report and an attestation request — their ledger,
their data, their disk. No account, no key, no upload, nothing to procure and
nothing for security to review, because nothing left.

  assurance kit init <folder>    a runnable kit: config, plan, README
  assurance kit check <config>   what a run would open. Reads only
  assurance kit run <config>     enrol every machine, offline

`check` is the command to put in front of whoever has to approve this: it names
every file the run would open and every plan item that matches no file, and
touches nothing. Exit codes are the interface — 0 quiet, 1 a finding, 2 could
not run. A refused connection attempt is a 1: the run is still valid, nothing
got out, but somebody needs to know which line tried.

assurance.kit.airgap is the part that makes the sales claim checkable rather
than promised. Every vendor says "nothing leaves your network"; plant IT has
heard it, cannot check it, and correctly treats it as worth nothing. This arms
a guard over the process's socket layer — connect, connect_ex,
create_connection, sendto, and getaddrinfo, because a tool that resolves a name
has already told a DNS server something — refuses every outbound connection,
records each attempt with the line of code that made it, and seals that record
into the customer's own ledger. The claim ends up as a hash in a file they hold.

Two design points that are decisions, not accidents:

  * AirgapError is not an OSError. Every HTTP client catches OSError as a
    transient failure: it would retry three times, or fall back to a cache and
    carry on as though nothing happened. A refusal here is not a network
    problem and must not be mistakable for one, so it travels up through every
    library's error handling untouched and stops the run.
  * The limits travel with the claim. The guard does not cover a subprocess
    (the kit spawns none), does not cover a C extension opening its own file
    descriptor, and records intent rather than enforcing containment —
    containment is a firewall's job. Those four sentences are in the evidence
    record and numbered in the report. A claim next to its limits is worth more
    to a reviewer than a broader claim with none.

Ordering inside a run, each step for a reason: collection and sealing happen
inside the guard; the airgap record is sealed after it releases, because a
record of a guard cannot be written while the guard it describes is running;
the run record is sealed before the head is read, so the attestation request
covers it. A head taken before the last append is stale the moment it is
written, and an attestation of a stale head looks like coverage and is not.

Refusals that protect a document nobody has written yet: the kit will not run
while organisation, prepared_by or site still hold a starter template value, or
while the plan says manufacturer "CHANGE ME". Those are printed at the top of a
report that goes to an auditor, and a manifest with a template manufacturer
cannot be matched to a supplier advisory later.

KitRun.verdict is deliberately not degraded by the standing caveat every
collection carries — that a plan only finds what it names. That sentence is
true of every run that will ever happen, so letting it force PARTIAL every time
would teach the reader the verdict means nothing, which is how a signal is
destroyed. It goes in what-was-not-checked. What moves the verdict is a machine
that did not enrol, a required item not found, or a connection attempt.

Every run ends by naming what it did not establish — no watch, no advisory
fan-out, no signature on the head, no physical behaviour, manifests read from
exports rather than machines. That list is the honest statement of the gap and
a better argument for the paid product than any claim about it: every line
names the thing that closes it.

ReportInput gains extra_limits, so a caller that knows something the engines
cannot — the airgap guard, the kit — joins the numbered limits section
verbatim like every other limit.

The kit is free, and in the free plan's entitlements: something that needed a
licence would need a procurement review, which is the thing it exists to avoid.

Tests: 32 new, 671 total, all passing. ruff clean, mypy clean on the new module.
The load-bearing ones point urllib at a real URL rather than calling connect by
hand, and run the whole commercial loop — offline enrolment, three numbers
counter-signed by a party that never saw the ledger, then a rewind of that
ledger caught by joining the two back together.

